  "rawBulletin": "REGIONAL SPECIALISED METEOROLOGICAL CENTRE -TROPICAL CYCLONES,\nNEW DELHI TROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO 28\nDEMS-RSMC TROPICAL CYCLONES NEW DELHI DATED 19.11.2026\nFROM: RSMC \u2013TROPICAL CYCLONES, NEW DELHI\nTO: STORM WARNING CENTRE, NAYPYITAW (MYANMAR)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, BANGKOK (THAILAND)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, COLOMBO (SRILANKA)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, DHAKA (BANGLADESH)\nSTORM WARNING CENTRE, KARACHI (PAKISTAN)\nMETEOROLOGICAL OFFICE, MALE (MALDIVES)\nOMAN METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT,\nMUSCAT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nYEMEN METEOROLOGICAL SERVICES,\nREPUBLIC OF YEMEN (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nNATIONAL CENTRE FOR METEOROLOGY, UAE (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nPRESIDENCY OF METEOROLOGY AND ENVIRONMENT,\nSAUDI ARABIA (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nIRAN METEOROLOGICAL ORGANISATION, (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nQATAR METEOROLOGICAL DEPARTMENT (THROUGH RTH JEDDAH)\nTROPICAL CYCLONE ADVISORY NO. 28 FOR THE NORTH INDIAN OCEAN\n(THE BAY OF BENGAL AND THE ARABIAN SEA) VALID FOR THE NEXT 168\nHOURS ISSUED AT 2300 UTC OF 19.11.2026 BASED ON 2100 UTC OF\n19.11.2026.\nSub: Cyclonic Storm \u201cAkvan\u201d [Pronunciation: Akvan] over the westcentral Bay of\nBengal [Cyclone Warning for North Andhra Pradesh & Yanam and South Odisha\ncoasts]\nThe Severe Cyclonic Storm \u201cAkvan\u201d [Pronunciation: Akvan] over the westcentral Bay of\nBengal moved north-northwestwards with the speed of 21 kmph during past 06 hours,\nweakened into a cyclonic storm and lay centered at 2100 UTC of 19th November, 2026 over\nthe same region, near Latitude 17.7\u00b0N and Longitude 84.9\u00b0E, about 110 km southeast of\nKalingapatnam (43105, Andhra Pradesh), 170 km east of Vishakhapatnam (43149, Andhra\nPradesh), 170 km south of Gopalpur (43049, Odisha), 250 km south-southwest of Puri\n(43053, Odisha), and 370 km east-northeast of Narsapur (43187, Andhra Pradesh).\nIt is likely to continue to move north-northwestward and cross North Andhra Pradesh and\nSouth Odisha coasts close to Gopalpur, by forenoon of 20th November, 2026 as a cyclonic\nstorm with the maximum sustained wind speed of 70-80 kmph gusting to 90 kmph.\nLatest Satellite imagery indicates the Land interaction commenced as the sheared cloud\nmass associated with the Cyclonic Storm \u201cAkvan\u201d entered into north Andhra Pradesh-south\nOdisha coasts.\nCloud distribution: (a) Isolated: <25%, Scattered:25-50%, Broken: 51-75%, Solid:>75%, Convection Intensity: (a) Weak:\nCloud Top Temperature(CTT)>-25\u00b0C, (b) Moderate: CTT:-25\u00b0Cto-40\u00b0C, (c) Intense: CTT: -41\u00b0Cto -70\u00b0C and (d) Very\nIntense::Less than -70\u00b0C Probability of cyclogenesis (formation of depression) :NIL:0%, LOW:1-33%, MODERATE:34-\n66% and HIGH:67-100%\nThe system is under continuous surveillance of the Doppler Weather Radar at Gopalpur and\nVishakhapatnam.\nThe forecast location and intensity of the system is given below:\nDate/Time Position Maximum sustained Category of cyclonic\n(UTC) (Lat. 0N/ long. surface disturbance\n0E) wind speed (Kmph)\n19.11.26/2100 17.7/84.9 80-90 gusting to 100 Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/0000 18.2/85.0 80-90 gusting to 100 Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/0600 19.3/85.0 70-80 gusting to 90 Cyclonic Storm\n20.11.26/1800 20.4/85.0 50-60 gusting to 70 Deep Depression\n21.11.26/0000 22.2/85.2 25-35 gusting to 45 Well Marked Low\nPressure Area\nAs per INSAT 3DS, vortex (AKVAN) lay over the Westcentral Bay of Bengal and its\nneighbourhood centered near 17.54N/84.9E. Intensity of the system is characterized as\nT3.0. Associated scattered to broken low and medium-level clouds, with embedded intense\nto very intense convection lay over North and Central Bay, North Odisha, Gangetic West\nBengal, north Costal Andhra Pradesh adjoining South Odisha (minimum Cloud-Top\nTemperature (CTT) ranges from -70\u00b0C to -90\u00b0C) and moderate to intense convection over\nsouth Bay, Jharkhand, east Bihar, Sub-Himalayan West Bengal, Sikkim, Northeast states of\nBangladesh and Bhutan (minimum CTT) ranges from -40\u00b0C to -70\u00b0C).\nThe estimated central pressure is 1001 hPa and maximum sustained wind speed is 45 kt\ngusting to 55 kt. High to very high Sea condition is prevailing over Bay of B",
